This is the hard version of the problem. The difference between the versions is that in this version, 1 <= i <= n - 1. Note that a correct solution for the hard version is not necessarily a correct solution for the easy version.
Given a positive integer n. Find some permutation p of length n such that for every i (1 <= i <= n - 1) there exists j (i <= j <= n) such that pi = pj XOR i, or determine that it does not exist.
A permutation of length n is an array consisting of n distinct integers from 1 to n in arbitrary order. For example, [2, 3, 1, 5, 4] is a permutation, but [1, 2, 2] is not a permutation (2 appears twice in the array), and [1, 3, 4] is also not a permutation (n = 3 but there is 4 in the array).

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 <= t <= 10^4). The description of the test cases follows.
The only line of each test case contains one positive integer n (3 <= n <= 2 * 10^5) — the length of the permutation.
It is guaranteed that the sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 2 * 10^5.

Output:
For each test case, if there is a suitable permutation, output n integers p1, p2, ..., pn — the permutation p. Otherwise, output -1.
If multiple solutions exist, output any of them.

Strategy:
This is like the easy version we just cannot just drop n off at idx=1. First we need to figure out for which is the problem unsolvable. This is for n that are a power of 2. This is because no matter what we xor them with (in the range 1, n) we either get 0 or a number >n which are both illegal. For these we output -1. For all of the other ns we can use the same solution as for the easy version. Make everything equal to 1 when xorred with its idx. The good thing about idx=1 is that if we put any other number than n there and xor it with 1 (which is the idx) the result must be in the rest of the array since it will either be num-1 or num+1 (-1 is not a problem since 1 is at the end just as in the easy version). This issue can be solved by just swapping n to any position which has a 1 at the same digit as n in the binary representation since this will make n smaller (we take the lsb and nothing else to not make it bigger). So, we create the array just as the easy version and then set the first position which shares a 1 with n to n and the first position to the number that was previously there. It is important to note we do this only if n is even since if n is odd then n^1 gives n-1 which is fine.